label start
[signal arg="bg:apartment_night"]
The victory screen had been up for eleven minutes.
He hadn't moved.
His right forearm ached the way it always did past midnight, a slow, deep pull along a ligament three surgeons had called fully healed.
It had been "fully healed" for four years. It still woke him at three in the morning.
It still would not close all the way around anything heavier than a coffee cup.
Lan (Sus): (The worst part was never the pain. The worst part was that I was winning.)
Two exchanges from the title match. A downward diagonal cut, a zornhau, thrown ten thousand times before.
His edge caught wrong. Then there was a sound, and then the ceiling.
Lan (Default): (One cheap practice sword, held for two seconds. Then my hand stopped being a hand.)
The monitor hummed.
100% COMPLETION ACHIEVED. All Secret Trophies and Character Routes Unlocked. Total Playtime, 2,450 hours.
Lan (Sus): (Four years of a life, turned into a number.)
Ritterland. Brutal, beautiful, and famous for being unfair.
A continent split by old wars, with the knight-scholars of Ritterland, the tower-mad empire of Britannic Arcana,
the star-reading courts of Al-Tale'a, the ice halls of Nordvinter, the island kings of Nusantara, and the quiet blades of Asahigawa.
Two powers run the world. Gi, the engine inside every living body.
Shir, that same energy pushed outside the body and shaped into fire, shields, illusions and healing.
And underneath it all, patient and sleeping, the Evil God, and the sickness called Gi Corruption that came with him.
Lan (Sus): (Every ending, every perfect run, the game demands payment. Friends die. Whole countries come off the map.)
Lan (Sus): (Nobody ever finds the version where nobody pays.)
Lan (Default): Another perfect save file.
No one was going to call. No one had called in a while.
Lan (Sus): (There's a kind of grief that's loud, and a kind that's just a very quiet apartment. I know which one I got.)
He reached for the mouse to shut it down. The screen glitched.
The color drained out of the victory banner, and red words surfaced one letter at a time.
SYSTEM NOTIFICATION, UNKNOWN SOURCE. You have cleared all standard scenarios. Yet the world's true collapse remains inevitable.
Would you like to unlock and reach the TRUE ENDING?
Lan (Sus): (There is no true ending. I read the raw files. I went through everything the developers cut. It isn't in the code.)
Lan (Sus): (A virus, a prank, or my graphics card dying.)
Lan (Sus): (What exactly am I protecting?)
label prompt
- YES
	jump ch00_prologue/yes
- NO
	He moved the cursor to NO. The button dimmed. The red text did not go away. It began to type again, slower, as if amused.
	WOULD YOU LIKE TO UNLOCK AND REACH THE TRUE ENDING?
	Lan (Sus): (It's not going to let me leave it alone.)
	jump ch00_prologue/prompt
label yes
Lan (Default): Fine.
He clicked. The hum climbed past what he could hear. Light poured out of the screen, and it had weight. It leaned into him.
The last thing he noticed was that, for the first time in four years, his right hand had closed all the way shut.
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch00"]
[end_timeline]
